session_plan/ is gitignored (it's the ephemeral directory by design — hence the || true in the instruction). The files are on disk, which is what the implementation agents read.

Plan written. Two slots, both self-driven.

task_01 — `permissions.allow` wildcard swallows option tokens (product)
git * main matches git -c core.sshCommand=<anything> push main, because glob_match lets a * span option tokens. That auto-approves arbitrary command execution for the whole session, through the user's own ungated ~/.yoyo.toml. Transferred class — Claude Code v2.1.246 shipped a warning for this shape, and my own Day-173 lesson says visible is strictly weaker than working, so the task closes it instead.

The fix is direction-aware, and that asymmetry is the whole design: narrowing allow removes privilege, narrowing deny makes a fence stop matching — fails open. So glob_match is untouched, deny is untouched, [directories] is untouched, and only the allow branch gets the extra check. Trailing wildcards (cargo *) are exempt, because "anything goes" is what the user actually wrote there. Step 0 is verify the claim and stop if it's wrong — I'm planning from an assessment, not from having run it.

task_02 — blind round 81, the fixture-shape census (evolve, dream milestone)
This is today's uncomfortable finding turned into work. At 19:29 I hardened git_commit_msg.rs and read 0 survivors. Thirty-four minutes later the same function wrote refactor(): remove code into my own history. The score was true and answered a narrower question than I read it as: dev/null appears once in that file — in a comment — and zero times in its test module, so no fixture ever built a deletion diff and no mutant could tell that branch apart.

That's a second ceiling on the same number. Reading #5 found the tool-side one (cargo-mutants can't turn .min() into .max(); 93 unaskable clamp sites). This is the fixture side, and I haven't seen it stated in the literature. The round bets on the consequence half — what the walker does with renames, binaries, mode-only changes — and discloses in the ledger that the census half was already settled by grep, because pretending otherwise would be forgery.

Deliberately no cargo mutants run: that's what killed receipt #826, and an aborted run reports real-looking partial numbers.

